1. ADMINISTRATIVE & PROTOCOL INFORMATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.1 Study Title:
Bridging the Implementation Gap: Intellectual Property Literacy, Perceived Institutional Readiness, and Innovation Practice among Pharmaceutical Professionals in Post-Liberation Syria

1.2 Principal Investigator & Institutional Affiliation:
Dr. Chadi Khatib, Department of Pharmacognosy, Faculty of Pharmacy, Manara University, Syria.

1.3 Ethical Approval:
Manara University Institutional Review Board (IRB Approval No. MU-URO-26-02). Electronic informed consent secured at platform entry.

1.4 Study Design:
Cross-sectional, observational, quantitative policy-oriented survey.

2. STUDY OBJECTIVES & HYPOTHESES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.1 Primary Objective:
To characterize the empirical relationships among IP literacy, professional innovation attitudes, perceived institutional readiness, and reported innovation practices among pharmaceutical personnel in post-liberation Syria (N = 303).

2.2 Formal Hypotheses:
- Hypothesis 1 (H1): Professional attitudes toward IP and innovation (IAI) show high baseline endorsement but remain statistically decoupled from reported innovation practice (IPI), demonstrating a substantial attitudinal-implementation gap.
- Hypothesis 2 (H2): Innovation readiness (IRS), conceptualized as a descriptive proxy for perceived institutional enabling conditions, exhibits structural floor effects that mirror the depression in reported innovation practice, consistent with macro-institutional operational constraints.
- Hypothesis 3 (H3): Reported innovation practice remains uniformly low across undergraduate students, practicing pharmacists, and academic researchers, demonstrating a structural floor effect without significant group variation.

3. SAMPLE SPECIFICATION & DEMOGRAPHICS (N = 303)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 Sample Size & Verification:
A total of 303 verified complete responses obtained from 400 distributed electronic invitations (75.83% response rate). Single-response restriction enforced via IP and browser tracking.

3.2 Sample Profile:
- Gender: Female = 215 (70.96%), Male = 88 (29.04%).
- Professional Category: Undergraduate Students = 224 (73.93%), Academic/Graduate Researchers = 54 (17.82%), Practicing Pharmacists = 25 (8.25%).
- Experience Level: <1 Year = 80 (26.40%), 1–5 Years = 153 (50.50%), 6–10 Years = 45 (14.85%), >10 Years = 25 (8.25%).

4. VARIABLE OPERATIONALIZATION (FORMATIVE COMPOSITE INDICES)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All constructs are conceptualized as Formative Composite Indices (FCIs) normalized to a 0–100 scale:
1. PILS_pct: Pharmaceutical IP Literacy Score (9 binary objective legal items, K2–K10).
2. IAI_pct: Intellectual Property Attitude Index (10 Likert items, A1–A10, reverse coding for A2, A5).
3. IRS_pct: Perceived Institutional Readiness Score (4 Likert items on TTOs, legal support, funding, R&D infrastructure, R1–R4).
4. IPI_pct: Innovation Practice Index (3 behavioral items on patents, R&D participation, technology transfer, P1–P3).
5. Diagnostic_Gap: Arithmetic difference (IAI_pct - IPI_pct).

5. STATISTICAL METHODOLOGY & ANALYTICAL PIPELINE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.1 Software & Environment:
Python 3.12 utilizing Pandas, NumPy, SciPy, and Statsmodels packages.

5.2 Descriptive Statistics & Zero-Inflation Diagnostics:
Calculation of Mean, SD, Median, IQR, Min, Max, Skewness, and exact count/percentage of zero scores to quantify floor effects.

5.3 Bivariate Correlation Analysis:
Non-parametric Spearman rank correlation coefficients (r_s) with Holm-Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons to account for non-normality and bounded 0–100 distributions.

5.4 Sectoral Group Comparisons:
One-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) and non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis test across professional categories (Students, Academics, Pharmacists) with effect size metrics (eta^2).

5.5 Multivariable Predictive Modeling (Handling Zero-Inflation):
- Tobit Regression Model (Left-censored at 0): Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) using GenericLikelihoodModel to account for 71.95% left-censoring in IPI.
- Binary Logistic Regression: Two-part model predicting any practice participation (IPI > 0).
- LOWESS Non-parametric Curve Estimation: Smooth locally weighted scatterplot regression to visualize threshold activation dynamics.

